job we know also that dr. cold award is  not in her job but your in your job  Dominic Cummings is in his job I do want  to ask you about something in The Times  today it says it seems extraordinary to  me that you've admitted that you showed  apparent bias in unlawfully intervening  to approve a planning decision in favor  of Richard Desmond's company in London  when the council and the inspector had  ruled against it so you admit you showed  apparent bias towards a Tory donor well  and so appropriate for me to get into  individual planning applications  obviously as housing secretary it's a  matter of record you have mitad  admitting apparent bias in this case and  what does that mean well I can assure  you that we took this decision as we do  all decisions on the merits we  absolutely refute any suggestion of bias  but we did wait a minute either you've  admitted a parent bias as reported in  The Times today or you haven't have you  admitted a parent bias no we said that  there there is no bias but that because  issues were raised for absolute fairness  we would quash the decision and enable  it to be retaken if that's what the  applicant wants and that will be retaken  as is the usual way by a different  Minister so that was done for complete  fairness and the decision if the  applicant wishes to do so can be retaken  at a later stage  well the applicant of course is the  person who benefits from this so I don't  think that's particularly meaningful  but this is being quoted I just want to  get this on the record have you admitted  officially this is what the times is  saying that you showed quotes apparent  bias in unlawfully intervening to  approve this planning application well  the the way that the court process works  is that if you want to retake the  decision then you have it quashed in the  court that's what that's what we've done  and that enables it to be retaken if the  applicant wishes that to be the case we  obviously don't believe that we acted  inappropriately or that we showed up I'm  just trying to establish the fight here  The Times is reporting not just read it  out to you that you have accepted that  you showed apparent bias in unlawfully  intervening to approve this application  by Richard Desmond's company a man who  supports the Conservatives and has  donated to the Conservatives I'm just  asking you factually in the legal  process have you admitted that you  showed apparent bias the legal process  sets out that the the way the decision  was taken could give rise to the  appearance of bias but obviously we  reject that there was any actual bias  and that's why for complete fairness the  department decided that the decision  could be retaken by a different Minister  and that may well happen if the  applicant wishes to do so in the future  because of course the reason why the  inspector and the counsel were against  it was because there wasn't enough  social housing enough housing for poor  people well I hope you'll respect this  so it's not appropriate me to get into  the details of individual your you've  taken a decision in this case so I think  it is appropriate just explain it is  true that the government is keen to get  more homes built and there's a  consistent record by this government and  indeed by Mia's secretary of states that  we've tried to ensure that homes get  built  particularly in London where development  has been too slow  in recent years so we have weren't  enough affordable homes social houses  for the people who need them there may  have been apartments for rich people who  live abroad and that may help mr.  Richmond mr. Desmond's company but the  issue is this is a typical example of a  scheme in London where it is said there  weren't enough social houses and you  have intervened to approve it if that's  the circumstance isn't it  well that isn't that isn't quite right  it's obviously it is more complicated  than that but it is correct that we  judged that the benefit of more homes  outweigh some of the other concerns with  the project more expensive so fewer  fewer cheaper homes and more expensive  homes across the country well we want to  get more homes of all kinds built  obviously in the right places and you  judge each application on its own merits  but we want to get more homes built of  all kinds we're doing that in a number  of different ways  supporting developments but also  investing in affordable housing as well  and we're bringing forward the largest  ever affordable homes program for  example it was announced at the budget  that will enable us to build thousands  more homes which are available to people  on low incomes we're also bringing  forward our signature homeownership  policy first homes that will give a 30%  discount to first-time buyers and to key  workers like nurses and care workers to  get their first step on the house just  one final question on this issue with  hindsight do you accept that you were  wrong to intervene and you shouldn't  have approved this development  well we judged it on the merits and that  was done without any actual bias but  clearly the way that the process was run  gave rise to some concerns and so that's  why we've chosen to quash the decision  by agreement with the other parties and  to have it read a term is the fairest  way to proceed now  now you've been widely praised for the  action the government's taken to deal  with the homeless homelessness cheering  this pandemic most of them I don't know  how many but how many people have been  taken into accommodation or off the  streets  well we've helped about 15,000 people  off the streets and into temporary but  safer accommodation like hotels and B&amp;Bs  to protect them from the pandemic and  that's probably saved hundreds of  people's lives but now there's a big  task ahead of us as to how we can ensure  that as few of those people as possible  end up back on the streets when the  lockdown measures are eased and so the  first step and