hat  that can then in time lead to the move  on accommodation that we're going to  bring forward as a result of this four  hundred million pounds investment and  this will be the largest investment in  move on accommodation for rough sleepers  we've ever had as a country the largest  since the rough sleeping initiative was  created by a predecessor of mine Sir  George Young in the early 1990s so it is  a big step forward but I don't  underestimate the scale of the challenge  ahead because we're dealing with  understandably complex individuals who  will need a lot of support to enable  them to to move forward I mean I  understand that but do you not think  that this crisis the way it's been  handled the success of it shows what  John Byrd and and other people are  saying for many years that are kind of  managed almost compulsory hostile  hostile program for the homeless people  is actually the way to eliminate the  problem  well I think it's only true that if you  can get people into accommodation you  know housing first as we call it that  that is the best way then to begin to  build up people and put the care around  them and that's the approach that we're  going to be taking forward now in this  whole reconstruction process which is  going to come after the lockdown whether  you as a minister are saying to the  Treasury look why don't we make some of  these hotels permanent and deal with the  problem that way because relative to the  cost of everything else furlough and all  the rest that would be quite low  wouldn't it well we've secured four  hundred and thirty million pounds to  bring forward these six thousand homes  and to ensure they've got the right  support around them we've also got a  hundred and twelve million pound a year  rough sleeping initiative so there are  quite large sums of money being invested  if we need to secure more funding then  obviously I'll be fighting for that with  the Treasury because there's matters to  us and it would be a huge missed  opportunity as a country if we allow  people to drift back onto the streets  unnecessarily it might be that we do  purchase some of the hotels that people  have been staying in but we've got to  make sure that we find safe good quality  accommodation that's right for the  individuals and that's exactly what  we're going to set out to do now in the  weeks ahead
 

